{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 36, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending as a new statute reinstates penalties for possessing small quantities of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth. Under HB4002, individuals caught with these drugs can face either criminal charges or an opportunity to enter a behavioral health initiative designed to steer them away from incarceration. Possession remains a misdemeanor, carrying a maximum sentence of half a year behind bars, while stricter consequences now apply to sales near areas like playgrounds and schools.\n\n***\n\nThe law promotes, but does not require, local jurisdictions to establish alternatives to arrest, offering services linked to mental health and substance support. Supporters argue this flexibility lets regions tailor responses to their capacity, while critics warn it could lead to inconsistent outcomes across areas. Police are instructed to offer a pathway to services only if the individual has no outstanding warrants, no history of violence, and is medically stable. If these conditions aren’t met, arrest becomes the default action.\n\n***\n\nWhen eligible, officers contact a regional coordination line to relay details and initiate a rapid response. A mobile health team must arrive within half an hour to assess and connect the person with aid. If the team cannot reach the scene in time, the individual will be taken into custody—police are not permitted to delay or linger. This tight timeline has raised concerns about whether adequate resources are in place to meet the demand.\n\n***\n\nIn a country where access to care remains uneven, the shift reflects a broader struggle to balance enforcement with compassion. One might wonder how many people, after a long day of shopping or simply trying to eat, end up caught 